WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
Can stimulating the brain create touch in a hand a person cannot feel?

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.220
And does it help?

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.770
In one man with a spinal cord injury, electrodes in sensory cortex evoked sensations

4
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:16.870
felt in the hand.

5
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:22.843
A responsive electrode evokes touch in a region of the hand, its projected field.

6
00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.103
Fields can overlap and change with stimulation.

7
00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:29.303
So each electrode is an approximate address.

8
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:31.937
Does feedback help a task?

9
00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:36.517
The same man moved a robotic arm, decoded from his motor cortex.

10
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:43.267
With touch feedback, his median task time fell from 20.9 seconds to 10.2.

11
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.990
Predict. By what percentage did his task time fall?

12
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.050
Choose your baseline first.

13
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:59.763
About 51 percent, measured against the time without feedback.

14
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:01.603
The working is on screen.

15
00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:04.726
But these are one person's medians.

16
00:01:05.566 --> 00:01:08.346
They show feedback helped him, not a population effect.

17
00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:11.866
Patterns across addresses can write features.

18
00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:17.326
In two people, several electrodes stimulated together evoked a tactile edge.

19
00:01:18.266 --> 00:01:19.886
Sequences evoked apparent motion.

20
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.940
This is the second half of the idea.

21
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.820
Send each group of neurons the right pattern.

22
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:31.113
One strategy is biomimicry, copying features of the natural code.

23
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:36.913
Here the scheme imitates cortical touch responses, strongest at contact onset and

24
00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:37.913
offset.

25
00:01:38.316 --> 00:01:43.156
In three people, biomimetic stimulation felt more like a real press on some

26
00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:47.536
electrodes. It also needed less charge for the same felt intensity.

27
00:01:48.616 --> 00:01:50.796
That supports a strategy with evidence.

28
00:01:51.716 --> 00:01:54.576
It is not a law that closer copying always works better.

29
00:01:55.599 --> 00:02:00.059
So map where each electrode's touch is felt, and test what the person can tell

30
00:02:00.059 --> 00:02:03.199
apart. Then send each group a pattern it can read.
